{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-004",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core endpoint routing route patterns constraints and binding",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Endpoint routing builds a set of endpoints, matches a request to candidates, applies constraints and policies, then selects the best endpoint. It does not execute routes one by one in declaration order, apart from documented MVC conventional-route ordering behavior.",
    "A route template describes literals, parameters, optional/default values, catch-alls, and constraints. Constraints help disambiguate URL shapes; they are not input validation and should not be used to return a client validation error.",
    "Route values are strings and endpoint selection is separate from parameter binding. Binding converts route, query, header, form, body, and service sources into action or handler parameters according to each programming model's rules.",
    "Attribute-routed endpoints and conventional MVC routes share endpoint routing infrastructure but use different conventions. URL generation uses endpoint metadata and values; successful inbound matching does not guarantee a desired outbound URL.",
    "Ambiguous candidates produce an error rather than relying on accidental registration order. Use explicit route design and inspect endpoint metadata when diagnosing matching or link-generation failures."
  ],
